Give eth0 a stable MAC and tell the router its name

Two things a router needs to show this board as anything but a hex string,
neither of which the stock firmware does.

The MAC is random on every boot. The device tree carries no local-mac-address
property, so the macb driver logs

    macb e000b000.ethernet: invalid hw address, using random

and eth0 comes up as a different locally-administered address each time. The
router therefore sees a brand new device at every reboot, hands out a fresh
lease, and a DHCP reservation cannot be made at all. Observed directly: two
consecutive boots took 192.168.129.139 and then .140. U-Boot already holds a MAC
in its environment (ethaddr) and uses it for its own networking, so eth0 is now
pinned to the same one and the board keeps a single identity from bootloader to
Linux. If ethaddr is unset the line is omitted and behaviour is unchanged.

Nothing was sent in the DHCP request to say who we are. udhcpc ran without a
hostname option, so DHCP option 12 was absent and a router had nothing to list
the board as except its MAC. busybox ifupdown turns a "hostname" line in the
interface stanza into "udhcpc -x hostname:", and a "hwaddress" line into an
"ip link set addr" before the interface comes up, so both fixes are two lines in
the file S40network already generates.

The default hostname becomes Fishball7020 rather than pluto, which is also the
mDNS name, so the board answers to Fishball7020.local. fw_setenv hostname still
overrides it per board - ./devkit net name <host> - and setting it back to
pluto restores compatibility with tooling that expects pluto.local.

diff --git a/buildroot/board/pluto/S40network b/buildroot/board/pluto/S40network
--- a/buildroot/board/pluto/S40network
+++ b/buildroot/board/pluto/S40network
@@ -22,6 +22,7 @@
 
 	ETH_IPADDR=`fw_printenv -n ipaddr_eth 2> /dev/null`
 	ETH_NETMASK=`fw_printenv -n netmask_eth 2> /dev/null || echo 255.255.255.0`
+	ETHADDR=`fw_printenv -n ethaddr 2> /dev/null`
 
 	WLAN_SSID=`fw_printenv -n ssid_wlan 2> /dev/null`
 	WLAN_PWD=`fw_printenv -n pwd_wlan 2> /dev/null`
@@ -47,10 +48,20 @@
 	if [ -n "$ETH_IPADDR" ]
 	then
 		echo -e "iface eth0 inet static" >> $IFAC
-		echo -e "\taddress $ETH_IPADDR\n""\tnetmask $ETH_NETMASK\n" >> $IFAC
+		echo -e "\taddress $ETH_IPADDR\n""\tnetmask $ETH_NETMASK" >> $IFAC
 	else
-		echo -e "iface eth0 inet dhcp\n" >> $IFAC
+		echo -e "iface eth0 inet dhcp" >> $IFAC
+		# Send a hostname in the DHCP request (option 12). Without it a router
+		# has no name to show and lists the board by MAC address.
+		echo -e "\thostname $HOSTNAME" >> $IFAC
 	fi
+	# The macb driver finds no MAC in the device tree - it logs "invalid hw
+	# address, using random" - so eth0 gets a NEW random MAC every boot. The
+	# router then sees a different device each time, hands out a different
+	# lease, and a DHCP reservation is impossible. U-Boot already has a MAC in
+	# its environment; use the same one so the board keeps one identity.
+	[ -n "$ETHADDR" ] && echo -e "\thwaddress ether $ETHADDR" >> $IFAC
+	echo "" >> $IFAC
 
 	### /etc/wpa.conf ###
 	if [ -n "$WLAN_SSID" ]
diff --git a/buildroot/configs/zynq_pluto_defconfig b/buildroot/configs/zynq_pluto_defconfig
--- a/buildroot/configs/zynq_pluto_defconfig
+++ b/buildroot/configs/zynq_pluto_defconfig
@@ -6,7 +6,7 @@
 BR2_TOOLCHAIN_EXTERNAL=y
 BR2_TOOLCHAIN_EXTERNAL_LINARO_ARM=y
 BR2_OPTIMIZE_2=y
-BR2_TARGET_GENERIC_HOSTNAME="pluto"
+BR2_TARGET_GENERIC_HOSTNAME="Fishball7020"
 BR2_TARGET_GENERIC_ISSUE="Welcome to Pluto"
 BR2_ROOTFS_DEVICE_CREATION_DYNAMIC_MDEV=y
 BR2_TARGET_GENERIC_ROOT_PASSWD="analog"
